{
  "abstract": "Neubig and Dyer (2016) computed count-based features for each fold from the other folds, because a network trained on the text its counts came from learns to trust them too much. We test that remedy for next-token count lists fed as an input feature to a 50M-parameter transformer at matched GPU training time, with registered predictions. Two half tables of order-2 top-16 lists are read either by the other half of the stream (out-of-fold) or by their own half (within-fold), so table size, keys and reader are identical. Out-of-fold beats within-fold by +11.33 mBPB at seed 44 and +12.55 [+12.43, +12.68] at seed 45, while arm levels move by up to 4.7 mBPB between seeds. Within-fold lists are worse than no lists on both seeds, with train-minus-validation gaps of -0.10 nats; out-of-fold gaps are zero. The contrast swaps all of a position's support, not just its own count. On CPU, deleting the position's own count lowers the within-fold excess stored target probability (+0.0089) by +0.0069, and also deleting the rest of its document lowers it by a further +0.00235; these are operational deletion effects, not estimates of model terms. Measured through the trainer's own lookup (backoff, average over orders), exposure is 0.0046 for greedy order-2 lists and 0.0109 for orders 2-4; across five exposed learned-reader runs, the gap per unit of exposure is -10 to -13 (post hoc). On top of learned bigram and count-allocated trigram tables (CAM), out-of-fold lists of orders 2-4 add +0.11 mBPB and orders 3-4 +0.47 on one seed, below the registered +0.8 criterion (a bounded negative for these configurations); greedy lists cost 20.0. An earlier count-basis study keeps only frequent histories and shows little measured exposure under our diagnostic; the cause of its negative remains unidentified.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Corrections requested in the acceptance review of Paper 39 v1. Text and annotations only; no numbers change.\n- Leave-one-out (Sections 2, 3.1, 5.1): leave-one-out statistics can retain target dependence or create a conditional\n  shift; under the fixed-q iid model the remaining n-1 visits have conditional expectation q_y; CatBoost's case is\n  cited as a possible opposite-sign shift, not a universal one.\n- Deletion identity (Section 4.3): stated for n > 1, with the code's convention (a zero denominator reads 0).\n- COUNT (Section 2): our lookup substitutes the lower-order list when a history is absent or has zero count, whereas\n  COUNT [1, Eq. 2] backs off for an unseen continuation; \"follow its reader and scoring\" is narrowed, and the CAM\n  negative is stated to be limited as evidence about COUNT itself.\n- Count-basis study (abstract, Claim 5, Section 5.4): \"not a leak\" is replaced by \"little measured exposure under\n  our diagnostic\".\n- Supplement: the leak_decomp.py docstring and outputs/registration_x1r.md now carry notes marking their earlier\n  interpretations (deletions as model terms; \"self-inclusion only\") as superseded. The ast_hash of leak_decomp.py is\n  unchanged; SOURCE.sha256 is regenerated.",
